WHY THIS WORKS
- separates "I don't provide donor egg services" from "I can't support you at all"
- does not talk her out of donor eggs or into them
- offers what is actually on the table instead of a consolation

CONVERSATION 1: asking for help arranging donor eggs

Lead: I've decided to go with donor eggs. Can you help me find a donor and get it organised?

Sonia: That part falls outside what I do. I'm a fertility coach, not a clinic. I don't arrange donors, run the treatment or manage any of the medical side. That's your clinic's work.

Lead: Oh. I thought you did the whole thing.

Sonia: I understand the confusion, it's a common one.

Where I can help is the part around it: your body being in the best state it can be for the transfer, and everything that affects whether a pregnancy holds once it starts. That side is still yours regardless of whose eggs are used.

Lead: I hadn't thought about it that way. I assumed once you use a donor it's out of your hands.

Sonia: The egg is. The uterus, the immune and metabolic environment, and how you go into it are not, and they matter.

Have you started with a clinic yet?

Lead: We have a consultation next month.

Sonia: Then keep that, it's the right first step and I'd never suggest otherwise.

If you want support on the other half of it, that's something I can help with. Have a look at my masterclass first so you know how I work before you decide anything.

{{masterclass_link}}

Lead: That's helpful, thank you. I'll come back after the consultation.

Sonia: Do. And good luck with it.

CONVERSATION 2: she was told donor eggs and is not ready

Lead: They told me donor eggs are my only option now. I'm 42 and I just can't accept that yet.

Sonia: That's a big thing to be told, and "only option" is a stronger phrase than most cases justify.

What was it based on: AMH, a cycle result, or age alone?

Lead: Two poor cycles and my AMH is 0.3.

Sonia: Then it's a serious picture, not a throwaway comment, and I'm not going to tell you they're wrong.

What I would want to know is whether anything besides the stimulation protocol was ever addressed between those two cycles.

Lead: Nothing was. Same everything, twice.

Sonia: Then there's a version of this you haven't tried yet. It isn't a promise of a different outcome. It just means you'd be deciding with everything done instead of something left undone.

Lead: That's exactly what I've been trying to explain to my husband.

Sonia: It's a reasonable position and it isn't denial.

I'd also say this honestly: donor eggs may still turn out to be the right answer, and nothing I do would make that a failure.

Lead: Would you work with me while I decide?

Sonia: I would. Get a call in first and let my team pull both cycles apart. They'll tell you straight whether there's real room or not.

